Step 1: Import required libraries
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

pandas (pd) → Used to create and work with tabular data (DataFrame).
matplotlib.pyplot (plt) → Used for creating charts and graphs.

Step 2: Create sample data
data = {'age': [25, 30, 35, 40, 22, 18, 50, 60, 45, 27, 33, 29, 36, 41]}

A dictionary is created.
Key is "age".
Value is a list of ages (survey respondents).

Step 3: Convert data into a DataFrame
df = pd.DataFrame(data)

Converts the dictionary into a pandas DataFrame.
Result looks like a table with one column:
age
25
30
35
...


Step 4: Set the figure size
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))

Defines the width and height of the chart.
10 → width
6 → height
Makes the graph clearer and more readable.

Step 5: Create the histogram
plt.hist(df['age'], bins=10, color='skyblue', edgecolor='black')

df['age'] → Uses the age column data.
bins=10 → Divides the age range into 10 groups (bars).
color='skyblue' → Bar color.
edgecolor='black' → Border color of bars.
📊 Histogram purpose:
Shows how frequently ages occur within certain ranges.

Step 6: Add a title
plt.title('Age Distribution of Survey Respondents')

Adds a heading to the chart.
Explains what the graph represents.

Step 7: Label the X-axis
plt.xlabel('Age')

X-axis represents age values.

Step 8: Label the Y-axis
plt.ylabel('Frequency')

Y-axis shows how many people fall into each age group.

Step 9: Display the plot
plt.show()

Renders and displays the histogram on screen.

✅ Final Output
A histogram showing age distribution
Helps analyze:
Most common age range
Spread of ages
Younger vs older respondents

